    "dailyBrief": {
        "brent": 86.61,
        "brentDeltaPct": -3.6,
        "bullets": [
            "Iran claims it still controls the Strait of Hormuz after a reported halt to U.S. bombing operations, per Marine Insight relaying an Iranian assertion not independently confirmed.",
            "Brent crude dropped 3.60% to $86.61 as of 14:52 UTC, pressured by unverified reports of renewed U.S.-Iran talks and diplomatic mediation signals.",
            "PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-07-23, recorded 10 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day."
        ],
        "date": "2026-07-28",
        "generatedAt": "2026-07-28T14:52:34.560Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains the center of an active sovereignty dispute. Marine Insight relayed an Iranian assertion of continued control over the waterway following a reported halt to U.S. bombing operations; that claim originates with Iranian authorities and has not been independently corroborated. Separately, our AIS tracking shows vessels anchored or stopped at 382 as of 14:52 UTC, and port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports reached 673 in the most recent 24 hours, a figure drawn from a different methodology and scope than the PortWatch transit count and not directly comparable to it. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-07-23, recorded 10 transits, against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day, a collapse Lloyd's List has characterized as tanker traffic in freefall. Brent fell 3.60% over 24 hours to $86.61 at 14:52 UTC, pulled lower by unverified reports of renewed U.S.-Iran talks, Oman's single-source proposal for joint strait control per Middle East Eye, and an unverified voluntary-fee framework floated via Egypt Independent. The Hormuz Index state composite sits in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as its top contributor. The forecast composite reads elevated but is easing over the same period, led by Polymarket escalation contracts. That divergence, an extreme present-state reading against an easing forward signal, captures the tension between entrenched physical disruption and diplomatic signals markets are beginning, cautiously, to price.",
